## 10. The highest level: what mastery actually means

If you want to become an exceptional student of maneuver warfare, I would organize your education around these ten intellectual capabilities:

1. Understand warfare as a complex adaptive system.
2. Understand the relationship between political objectives and military strategy.
3. Recognize the difference between tactical success and strategic success.
4. Understand operational art and campaign design.
5. Study how command systems function under uncertainty.
6. Understand the role of logistics in military history.
7. Analyze why historical campaigns succeeded or failed.
8. Understand the interaction of technology, geography, and military institutions.
9. Study how armies learn, adapt, and innovate.
10. Develop the ability to critically evaluate competing military theories.

The most important distinction: Maneuver warfare is not simply moving faster or surrounding an opponent. It is a theory of achieving military and political objectives through the intelligent use of time, space, information, organization, and initiative.

I would study it through historical analysis, professional military education, and command simulations rather than treating historical battles as recipes to reproduce.

